00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.200
learning.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.200
Ali, thank you so much.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.650
Thank you, Jack, for that introduction.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.050
And thanks also to Crystal for organizing
all these efforts.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:36.560
I also wanted to thank Michelle Dunn, who
spearheaded many of the efforts on the BD2K

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:44.350
training activities and was key in the success
of BD2K training activities.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:49.420
So as Jack mentioned, I'm going to talk about
unsupervised machine learning techniques today.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.330
And this is one of the main two classes of
statistical learning along with supervised

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.170
machine learning, which was the topic of last
week's webinar.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:04.479
In supervised machine learning, as some of
you saw last week, we use a data set, x, to

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:08.450
predict or detect associations with response,
y.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.099
Whereas in unsupervised machine learning,
there is no response y.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:16.349
So we're only looking at a dataset, x, and
we're trying to identify patterns or signal

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:17.390
in that dataset.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.980
And that's the topic of today's webinar.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.620
I should also mention that there are other
categories of statistical machine learning-semisupervised

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.010
learning, which is somewhere between supervised
and unsupervised, and reinforcement learning,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.550
but we will not talk about those today.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:35.959
So to better understand what unsupervised
machine learning tries to do, it's helpful

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:38.890
to look at supervised learning, which is more
familiar.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:46.200
This is a classical supervised learning task
where we have observations in the space of

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.430
features, x1, x2.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.790
So we have two features here.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:55.960
These could be abundances of two proteins
or activities measured at two different brain

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:56.960
regions.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.600
And each dot in this plot corresponds to one
subject, let's say.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.840
So in addition to having the measurements
of different subjects with these two features,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:13.390
we also have the labels associated with each
subject.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.970
And so in this case, we have two labels, blue
and orange.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:28.180
And so a classical supervised learning task
would be to classify the observations based

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.489
on the feature space.

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:37.860
So an example would be to define a support
vector classifier, which would be a hyperplane

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.110
that would distinguish the blue and orange
classes.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:46.840
And then we could use this classifier that
we learned, let's say, based on some training

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.269
data in order to predict the outcome of future
observations.

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:57.069
The main difference from supervised learning
in the setting of unsupervised learning is

00:03:57.069 --> 00:04:00.990
that we still have the same data, we still
have the same features x1 and x2, but we no

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.670
longer have the labels for the classes.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:09.030
So instead of having the setting where we
have blue and orange points, we just have,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.069
let's say, gray points.

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:12.500
We don't know what these points correspond
to.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.709
All we know is that we have measurements of
x1 and x2.

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:19.019
So we can't, for example, do classification
anymore.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:23.030
However, we could still think about whether
there are groupings in the data.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:25.410
So this would correspond to a clustering task.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:30.050
Or whether there are lower dimensional representations
that could capture information in the data.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:33.590
Or whether there are associations between
the features, for example, in this case, x1

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:39.869
and x2, that would be meaningful and would
help us to identify some patterns in the data.

00:04:39.869 --> 00:04:43.909
So to summarize, the main difference between
supervised learning and unsupervised learning

00:04:43.909 --> 00:04:50.110
is that, in supervised learning, we have a
data matrix, x, which is an n by p array of

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.349
data corresponding to p measurements for each
of n subjects.

00:04:53.349 --> 00:04:55.469
In the previous example, p was 2.

00:04:55.469 --> 00:04:57.669
We had two measurements in general.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:04:58.669
We could have more.

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:04.889
And in biological applications, especially
in biomedical big data, we usually have p

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:08.539
in the order of thousands or hundreds of thousands.

00:05:08.539 --> 00:05:15.150
And y is a vector of outcome or response for
each one of the subjects.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:20.379
In unsupervised learning, we still have the
same data matrix, x, but we no longer have

00:05:20.379 --> 00:05:23.619
a vector of outcome or response.

00:05:23.619 --> 00:05:30.059
And all we are doing is to look at the data
matrix in order to identify patterns or signal

00:05:30.059 --> 00:05:31.120
that is interesting.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.789
Now, why do we use unsupervised learning given
that there's no association with the outcome?

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:41.500
Well, unsupervised learning is an important
part of the data analysis pipeline.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:48.499
In fact, unsupervised learning is used commonly
in the exploratory data analysis phase.

00:05:48.499 --> 00:05:53.399
Along with data wrangling and data visualization,
a lot of times unsupervised learning techniques

00:05:53.399 --> 00:05:57.159
are used to aid data wrangling and data visualization.

00:05:57.159 --> 00:06:02.509
And sometimes, they are used to generate new
hypotheses that we could then validate in

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:03.589
independent data.

00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:09.159
And this is when we basically identify interesting
patterns and signal in our data in order to

00:06:09.159 --> 00:06:10.189
generate hypotheses.

00:06:10.189 --> 00:06:14.669
Unsupervised learning methods are also used
in conjunction with supervised learning techniques

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:16.659
and confirmatory data analysis.

00:06:16.659 --> 00:06:23.610
So Brian Caffo gave a really nice explanation
of exploratory versus confirmatory data analysis

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:28.009
a couple of weeks ago at one of these webinars.

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:36.429
And as he pointed out, oftentimes analyses
are somewhere in this spectrum of exploratory

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:38.139
data analysis to confirmatory data analyses.

00:06:38.139 --> 00:06:40.249
There are analyses that are purely exploratory.

00:06:40.249 --> 00:06:43.379
There are analyses that are purely confirmatory.

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:46.189
And there analyses that fall somewhere in
between the two.

00:06:46.189 --> 00:06:51.800
And for many of the analysis that falls somewhere
between the two, unsupervised learning is

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.289
used extensively.

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:59.009
It is important to keep in mind that when
we use data to generate hypotheses, and then

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:05.439
later on test those hypotheses, if we are
using the same data, the findings of our study

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:06.770
are still exploratory.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:11.139
We cannot make strong claims based on what
we find partly because these might not be

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:12.139
reproducible.

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:18.209
We drive the hypotheses based on the same
data, and we have, in essence, validated the

00:07:18.209 --> 00:07:21.149
same hypotheses using the same data.

00:07:21.149 --> 00:07:27.300
If we want to ensure the reproducibility of
results, we should instead use, for example,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.619
a training test approach that was discussed
in last week's webinar or a validation-centered

00:07:31.619 --> 00:07:32.619
approach.

00:07:32.619 --> 00:07:34.180
For example, we should use crossvalidation.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.180
And we should do that on the whole pipeline.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.860
So all the steps from data wrangling, data
visualization, unsupervised learning, which

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:46.460
might generate some hypotheses, all the way
to supervised learning that would verify these

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:52.139
hypotheses or look for association with the
outcome-- they should be validated in independent

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:58.279
data or in held out dataset in order to make
sure that the results are reproducible.

00:07:58.279 --> 00:08:02.739
And if this is not possible, then we should
interpret the results as an exploration of

00:08:02.739 --> 00:08:03.739
the data.

00:08:03.739 --> 00:08:09.379
We should not get potentially too excited
about what we are observing in our data.

00:08:09.379 --> 00:08:17.069
So with that introduction, we'll focus on
two of the main classes of unsupervised learning

00:08:17.069 --> 00:08:19.050
methods throughout today's webinar.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.599
We will focus on clustering techniques and
dimension reduction.

00:08:21.599 --> 00:08:26.459
We will not talk about assocation rule mining
and graphical modeling network analysis, which

00:08:26.459 --> 00:08:30.709
although interesting, we won't have time to
cover them in this lecture.

00:08:30.709 --> 00:08:35.600
Clustering, of course, is widely used in analysis
of biomedical big data.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.190
Here's a very well-known example of one of
the pioneering applications of clustering

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:46.540
in analysis of gene expression data, where
[INAUDIBLE] and others used clustering techniques

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:48.910
to identify subtypes of breast cancer.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:56.740
And they later validated these clusterings
and verified them-- these subtypes-- and they

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.810
used them to define effective therapies.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:06.230
Now, in clustering, the main objective is
somewhat vague.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:11.600
The objective of clustering is to group the
objects into meaningful clusters such that

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.080
objects within each cluster are more similar
to one another than objects in other clusters.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:23.879
Now, the reason that this is somewhat vague
is that, depending on how we define meaningful

00:09:23.879 --> 00:09:26.040
clusters or
meaningful subsets, we could have different

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.600
number of clusters and we could get different
clusterings in our data.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.730
Here's a simple example.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:38.050
Depending on how we define what a meaningful
cluster is, we might get different number

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:39.870
of clusters.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:45.910
One might reasonably say that there are two
clusters in this dataset, separating them

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:47.910
into red and blue.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:52.610
Another person could also reasonably look
at this dataset and say there are six different

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:53.610
clusters.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.220
And this just depends on how we define clusters--
how we define similarity within groups and

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:01.019
difference outside of these groups.

00:10:01.019 --> 00:10:04.420
Even someone might look at this data and say,
well, there are four clusters.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.300
All of this is dependent on how we define
meaningful clusters.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.070
So this is one of the complications of clustering.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:18.089
Similarly, the way that we define similarity
among objects has consequences in terms of

00:10:18.089 --> 00:10:19.610
what we mean by meaningful concepts.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:24.720
So here, for example, we could consider two
clusters defined based on two curves in the

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.690
data or two manifolds in the data.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.910
These would be the red and blue clusters.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:34.660
Or someone might reasonably use a different
notion of similarity in the observations and

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.529
define different clusters.

00:10:36.529 --> 00:10:43.490
So these are issues that are important and
need to be decided throughout different clustering

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:44.490
tasks.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:49.560
And different choices would result in different
clustering outcomes.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.081
In addition to deciding what we mean by meaningful
cluster, we should also decide what we want

00:10:54.081 --> 00:10:55.081
to cluster.

00:10:55.081 --> 00:10:57.709
We have a couple of different options.

00:10:57.709 --> 00:10:59.009
We could cluster observations.

00:10:59.009 --> 00:11:02.150
This is the most commonly used option.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:09.850
And for example, in the paper that I showed
you, clustering was used in order to identify

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:10.990
subtypes of breast cancer.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.589
The samples were clustered in order to identify
subtypes of breast cancer.

00:11:14.589 --> 00:11:19.180
We could also cluster the features.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:26.519
This is used extensively when we want to find
genes that have similar functions, for example,

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:29.850
in order to identify proxies to biological
pathways.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:35.459
In fact, cluster both-- we could cluster observations
and features together.

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:39.079
And this is often referred to as bi-clustering.

00:11:39.079 --> 00:11:43.990
Throughout this lecture, given the similarities
of these tasks, we will focus only on clustering

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:45.939
of observations.

00:11:45.939 --> 00:11:51.420
But same techniques and same issues that we
will discuss here will also come up in clustering

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.279
of features or in biclusters.

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:59.220
Cause So today, we will talk about two classes
of clustering techniques, hierarchical methods

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.339
and partition based methods.

00:12:00.339 --> 00:12:04.420
And we will cover an example of each one of
these briefly.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:08.610
We will talk with hierarchical clustering
and we will talk about k-means clustering.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:16.220
Hierarchical clustering is basically a way
to get a sequence of nested clusters that

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:20.749
are organized in a tree structure, which is
referred to as a dendrogram.

00:12:20.749 --> 00:12:23.709
And there are two classes of hierarchical
clustering methods.

00:12:23.709 --> 00:12:29.199
We could build these trees in a bottom up
fashion starting with n individual clusters

00:12:29.199 --> 00:12:34.230
and then merging them until we get 1 cluster
that contains all the observations.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.930
Or we could do it in a top down way.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:39.709
We could start with one cluster and use it
in a measure of distance in order to

00:12:39.709 --> 00:12:45.430
separate the clusters further down and get
n separate clusters.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.540
And these, again, are similar somewhat.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:52.329
We will focus on bottom up approaches here
in this lecture.

00:12:52.329 --> 00:12:58.970
So to explain the main idea of hierarchical
clustering, consider this simple example with

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:04.350
nine observations plotted on the space of
two features, feature one and feature two.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:12.300
Now, when we use hierarchical clustering,
we get a dendrogram, which is a tree on the

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:18.939
left that explains how merging should occur
between observations, how observations should

00:13:18.939 --> 00:13:20.560
be clustered together.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:28.990
And for example, we could easily see that
in this dendrogram, observations 5 and 7 are

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:30.170
merged together.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:36.079
And then they're merged with observation 8,
and then merged with observation 2, and then

00:13:36.079 --> 00:13:37.660
later 9.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:39.550
And on the other hand, 6 and 1 are merged
together.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.100
And they are merged with 4, merged with 3.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.589
And then at the very end, the two groups are
merged together.

00:13:44.589 --> 00:13:47.640
Now, how are these dendrograms formed?

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:53.029
Basically, we should use some notion of distance
or similarity in our dataset in order to form

00:13:53.029 --> 00:13:54.490
these dendrograms.

00:13:54.490 --> 00:14:01.709
So going back to our plot of data points,
we see that 5 and 7 are very similar to each

00:14:01.709 --> 00:14:03.199
other-- very close to each other on this plot.

00:14:03.199 --> 00:14:07.170
So those are the first two that have merged
to form a cluster.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.779
6 and 1 are the next two closest points.

00:14:10.779 --> 00:14:14.639
Then those are merged together and they form
a cluster.

00:14:14.639 --> 00:14:19.180
The next cluster that is formed is by joining
8 to 5 and 7.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.260
So that's another cluster.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:24.839
And then the next cluster would be adding
4 to 6 and 1.

00:14:24.839 --> 00:14:32.759
So you see that as we go up the tree, you're
forming-- on this vertical axis, we're forming-we're

00:14:32.759 --> 00:14:35.050
merging clusters to form bigger and bigger
clusters.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:43.790
And at the very top of the tree, always we'll
get one cluster that contains all the points.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:45.430
What do we need to make a dendrogram?

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:49.750
Well, we need at least two pieces of information.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:54.040
One, we need a similarity of distance between
observations.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:58.380
As we discussed in the previous example, we
need to know how far the observations are

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.380
from each other.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:02.569
Now, we don't need to necessarily have a plot
of observations, but we need to have some

00:15:02.569 --> 00:15:04.930
notion of distance or similarity between observations.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:10.339
But in addition to that, we also need a measure
of similarity or distance between clusters.

00:15:10.339 --> 00:15:19.540
As I mentioned, as we go up the tree, in a
lot of cases, further up, we merge sets together.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:24.149
And in those cases, we have to think about
similarities between sets or distances between

00:15:24.149 --> 00:15:25.149
sets.

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:26.779
And this is what is known as a linkage function.

00:15:26.779 --> 00:15:39.119
So this simple example with two clusters with
two and three observations in each explains

00:15:39.119 --> 00:15:40.290
the notion of a linkage function.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.160
There are three different choices that we
will discuss.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:47.370
One is that in order to define a distance
between the two sets, we take the minimum

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.730
distance between the observations in the two
sets-- between the points of the two sets.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.290
And that is known as a single linkage function.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:58.640
The opposite is to take the maximum distance
between the observations of two sets.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:01.449
And that would give us a complete linkage.

00:16:01.449 --> 00:16:08.980
And then we also have the option of taking
the average of the distances between the points

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.420
in all the points in the two sets.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:12.869
And that would give us the average linkage
function.

00:16:12.869 --> 00:16:17.290
There are, of course, a few other sources
of linkage function, but we will not discuss

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:21.480
those in today's lecture.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:24.420
So we mentioned that there are three different
choices of linkage function.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.240
Which one of these should we use?

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:32.940
The answer is that there is no best linkage
function and that the choice of linkage function

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:38.029
that is appropriate depends on characteristics
of data that are unfortunately, a lot of times,

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:39.800
unknown to us.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:48.199
So to give you a summary, single linkage function
works best when there aren't many outliers

00:16:48.199 --> 00:16:50.579
or the data is not very noisy.

00:16:50.579 --> 00:16:54.160
But it's capable of identifying clusters with
diverse shapes.

00:16:54.160 --> 00:17:00.410
On the other hand, complete linkage and average
linkage to some degree are more robust to

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:06.120
presence of outlier and noise in the data,
but they require clusters that are roughly

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:08.170
comparable in size.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.800
And they work better with spherical distributions.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:17.230
So if we have clusters that have the irregular
shapes, we might not be able to distinguish

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:19.659
them well with complete linkage or average
linkage.

00:17:19.659 --> 00:17:23.220
And here are some examples that would explain
hopefully some of these issues.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:32.679
So if we have outliers as is the case in this
data set where we have observations that fall

00:17:32.679 --> 00:17:37.360
somewhere between the two main clusters, then
single linkage clustering might have difficulty

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.780
separating the two clusters from each other.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:46.500
And instead, they might identify one point
as one cluster and all the other observations

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:51.260
as another cluster, whereas complete linkage
and average linkage do a better job of separating

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:55.860
the two clusters from each other because they're
less prone to presence of outliers.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:03.630
And of course, in reality, you wouldn't know
which one of the two clusterings is correct--

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:08.090
whether complete linkage is giving the right
answer or average linkage.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:13.510
The main difference is these few points, or
in this case, this is simulated data, so I

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:17.919
do know which one is correct, and complete
linkage is doing the correct clustering.

00:18:17.919 --> 00:18:22.890
But in general, we wouldn't know the difference
between.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:29.179
As I mentioned, single linkage is better at
capturing clusters that have nonspherical

00:18:29.179 --> 00:18:30.179
shapes.

00:18:30.179 --> 00:18:31.370
Here's a good example of that.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:35.970
At the single linkage, especially if we don't
have-- if we have some separation between

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:41.850
clusters, we could distinguish clusters that
are embedded and, in this case, nested-- these

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:45.590
embedded clusters within each other, whereas
complete linkage and average linkage won't

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:52.039
be able to separate these two clusters properly
and distinguish them from each other.

00:18:52.039 --> 00:18:57.299
And the last example shows that the setting
when we have clusters that have very different

00:18:57.299 --> 00:18:58.299
sizes.

00:18:58.299 --> 00:19:00.620
In this case, complete linkage could break
down.

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:04.950
It's the most sensitive to having clusters
of roughly equal size.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:09.280
In this case, as you see, it forces them--
two clusters that identify to have roughly

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:13.590
equal size whereas single linkage and average
linkage do a better job of separating these

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.710
two clusters from each other.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:21.669
Now, all of these examples that I showed you
were in the simple setting that we had two

00:19:21.669 --> 00:19:23.399
feature so we could plot the observations.

00:19:23.399 --> 00:19:30.570
Of course, in the analysis of biomedical big
data, we often can't plot the observations.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:35.200
And we have no sense of what the shape of
the true clusters are.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:40.431
So finding the right choice of linkage function
and, in fact, even finding the right choice

00:19:40.431 --> 00:19:47.460
of distance between or similarity between
observations is often challenging.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:55.149
Now, going back to the dendrogram, as we discussed,
at the bottom of the dendrogram, we have a

00:19:55.149 --> 00:19:57.950
leaf for each one of the observations.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:03.830
And as we go up the tree, these leaves get
fused to form branches.

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:09.390
Now, I want to emphasize the interpretation
of the dendrogram here.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:12.070
And there are a couple of important things
that I want to point out.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:19.929
One is that the earlier the points are fused
together, the earlier the fusions occur, the

00:20:19.929 --> 00:20:22.789
more similar the observations of the clusters
are to each other.

00:20:22.789 --> 00:20:26.640
So the fusions at the bottom of the tree indicate
that these observations are very similar to

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:30.230
each other, whereas fusions that have happened
later on indicate that these observations

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.200
are not based on [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:39.669
So that's an important piece of information
that the dendrogram provides us.

00:20:39.669 --> 00:20:44.529
One thing in that goes hand-in-hand with that
is that the vertical axis in dendrogram is

00:20:44.529 --> 00:20:46.500
very informative.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:55.110
The height of the first fusion between clusters
containing two different observations corresponds

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:56.930
to how different they are from each other.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:21:03.380
So if you look at these two observations,
for example, they are fused at the very bottom

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:04.380
of the cluster.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:08.620
So that indicates that the distance between
these two observations is very small, whereas

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:16.450
if you consider this leaf and another leaf
in this branch, for example here, their corresponding

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:18.920
clusters are merged much higher in the graph.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:23.929
So the distance between those two clusters
is around 7.

00:21:23.929 --> 00:21:32.149
So there is a significant difference between
points that are merged earlier in the dendrogram

00:21:32.149 --> 00:21:34.830
than points that are merged higher in the
dendrogram.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:38.049
And the vertical axis provides us that information.

00:21:38.049 --> 00:21:44.360
On the other hand, the horizontal axis is
not informative at all in the dendrogram.

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:48.740
So we should not interpret the horizontal
axis in any informative way.

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:51.059
And the prime example of that are these two
leaves.

00:21:51.059 --> 00:21:53.340
Hopefully, you guys see my cursor.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:58.110
Consider this observation, this leaf, and
the one that is very adjacent to it in this

00:21:58.110 --> 00:21:59.110
leaf.

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:03.890
And even though these are adjacent to each
other on the horizontal axis, they're in fact

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:08.870
the last two observations to get merged at
the very top of the tree.

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:14.110
So they can't be any more different from each
other in this example.

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:23.090
So the observation at the horizontal axis
and the layout of the leaves on the horizontal

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:24.760
axis is somewhat arbitrary, and you should
not

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:30.559
interpret that in an informative way.

00:22:30.559 --> 00:22:39.190
Now, depending on where we cut this dendrogram
or where we cut this tree, we get different

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:40.480
number of clusters.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:45.549
So for example, we could cut the tree somewhere
around here.

00:22:45.549 --> 00:22:51.059
And then we will get two clusters, these red
and green clusters.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:59.160
So anywhere from the very top to the distance
7, we get two clusters.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:08.850
If we cut the dendrogram further towards the
bottom, we get more and more clusters.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.570
So if we cut the dendrogram some where here,
we get three clusters.

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:17.900
And if we cut it further down, we get additional
number of clusters identified.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:27.640
Now, one thing to keep in mind and is important
is that, as we discussed, fusions that occur

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:32.930
earlier in the dendrogram indicate more similar
clusterings.

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:41.370
So if we cut the dendrogram further down,
we are cutting into groups of observations

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:43.220
that are more similar with each other.

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:47.309
So ideally you want to look for gaps in where
the fusions occur.

00:23:47.309 --> 00:23:50.820
So in this example, cutting it at 2 or 3 is
sensible.

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:53.289
3 is probably the best choice.

00:23:53.289 --> 00:23:57.230
But cutting it to get 2 or 3 dendrograms is
sensible.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:24:02.389
But cutting it to get more clusters is not
very reasonable because then we're cutting

00:24:02.389 --> 00:24:07.370
into clusters that are somewhat homogeneous
inside them.

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:14.590
Now, let's look at how these dendrograms would
look like in a somewhat more realistic setting.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:18.110
And this is the clustering of NCI60 data.

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:22.919
There are 64 cancer cell lines.

00:24:22.919 --> 00:24:28.590
And in each one of them, about 6,800 genes
were measured.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:33.409
And if you use complete linkage, average linkage,
and single linkage, dendrograms that we get

00:24:33.409 --> 00:24:40.200
are somewhat very different than dendrograms
I showed you in that simpler example.

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:46.029
For one, the clusterings in all of these dendrograms
are less clear.

00:24:46.029 --> 00:24:49.639
There is less separation between the leaves-between
the clusters.

00:24:49.639 --> 00:24:58.210
And then all the leaves are somewhat combined
towards the top of the tree.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:01.690
With complete linkage, we get a little bit
more separation towards the top.

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:09.169
But with average linkage and single linkage,
basically, we don't have good separation between

00:25:09.169 --> 00:25:10.169
the clusters.

00:25:10.169 --> 00:25:14.320
Another feature of these high dimensional
settings that we see in this example is the

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.320
behavior of single linkage clustering.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:23.659
And this is often due to the noise that is
induced because of the high dimensionality.

00:25:23.659 --> 00:25:28.940
In a lot of high dimensional examples, single
linkage is not capable of identifying meaningful

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:37.399
clusters, and what we see is that clusters
are formed by adding one observation at a

00:25:37.399 --> 00:25:38.399
time.

00:25:38.399 --> 00:25:44.220
So as you go higher on the tree, one observation
is added to the previous cluster, and that

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:45.460
cluster gets bigger.

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:48.349
That's the dominant pattern that we see.

00:25:48.349 --> 00:25:52.409
And this is often referred to as chaining
behavior of single linkage clustering.

00:25:52.409 --> 00:25:57.409
So in a lot of high dimensional applications,
single linkage, despite its appealing properties

00:25:57.409 --> 00:26:07.779
to detect clusters with diverse shapes, is
not very effective in high dimensions.

00:26:07.779 --> 00:26:10.019
We discussed briefly hierarchical clustering.

00:26:10.019 --> 00:26:13.409
It's an easy to interpret method.

00:26:13.409 --> 00:26:15.270
It gives a family of nested clusters.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:17.539
It's computationally rather efficient.

00:26:17.539 --> 00:26:21.820
And it also works in high dimensions with
the caveat that we just discussed.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:30.919
The disadvantages of hierarchical clustering
is that it doesn't really correspond to a

00:26:30.919 --> 00:26:32.450
formal optimization criteria.

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:37.110
It doesn't achieve anything in any formal
objective.

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:42.179
And the final solution is chosen by the data
scientists or the analysts.

00:26:42.179 --> 00:26:45.070
And that could be somewhat arbitrary.

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:50.149
And as we discussed, different merging or
splitting criteria could give rise to different

00:26:50.149 --> 00:26:53.500
solutions.

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:59.200
In contrast to hierarchical clustering and
hierarchical methods, k-means clustering,

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:04.940
which is an example of partition based methods,
tries to find the best partition of the observations

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.830
into a priori defined number of clusters.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:12.250
So we first consider a sort of-- that we want
to partition our observation to k clusters.

00:27:12.250 --> 00:27:14.090
And k is given.

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:19.380
And then k-means clustering tries to partition
the observations in the best possible way

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:20.620
into these number of clusters.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:24.970
It has a simple and intuitive objective function.

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:26.750
And it's computationally really rather efficient.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:29.950
It could be applied to data sets with a large
number of observations.

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:34.090
However, the main drawback is that there is
no hierarchy among clusters.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:39.380
If you change k, for example, from 2 to 3--
that instead of wanting 2 clusters, now you

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:43.600
want 3 clusters, there's no guarantee that
we have a hierarchy among clusters.

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:47.250
So the clustering memberships might completely
change.

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:53.460
To explain the k-means clustering, I briefly
would like to discuss its algorithm partly

00:27:53.460 --> 00:27:59.549
because the actual optimization function that
is associated with k-means clustering is not

00:27:59.549 --> 00:28:00.549
tractable.

00:28:00.549 --> 00:28:02.159
Finding the exact solution to that is not
tractable.

00:28:02.159 --> 00:28:08.900
On the other, hand, we could find very good
approximate solutions using a simple algorithm.

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:13.549
The idea is that we first randomly assign
each one of the observations to one of k clusters.

00:28:13.549 --> 00:28:19.559
And then we iterate two steps until the clustering
memberships don't change anymore.

00:28:19.559 --> 00:28:21.760
First we calculate cluster centroids.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:24.559
This is the mean of the observations in each
one of the clusters.

00:28:24.559 --> 00:28:26.980
And then we assign cluster memberships.

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:30.899
We assign each point to the centroid that
is closest to it.

00:28:30.899 --> 00:28:33.250
So here's an illustration of that procedure.

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:34.559
This is a dataset.

00:28:34.559 --> 00:28:37.279
Suppose that we want to cluster it into three
clusters.

00:28:37.279 --> 00:28:44.820
First, we randomly split the observations
into one of three clusters, red, blue, and

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:45.820
green.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:49.650
And then we just simply calculate the centroids,
the mean of the red, that mean of the blue,

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:50.650
and the mean of the green.

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:54.360
Of course, in this case, these means are not
that different from each other.

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:58.580
But nonetheless, we could now assign each
one of the observation to a centroid that

00:28:58.580 --> 00:28:59.960
is closest to it.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:03.990
So now, we get these three clusters in the
data.

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:07.740
And in fact, if we continue this process,
then you would

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:10.740
recalculate the centroids.

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:15.580
And then even after 10 iterations, we don't
get any changes.

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:21.080
So in this somewhat toy example, we get the
partitions very quickly just by one pass of

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:22.080
the k-means clustering algorithm.

00:29:22.080 --> 00:29:31.299
And in many examples, this algorithm converges
very quickly, and we get the results of a

00:29:31.299 --> 00:29:35.549
good partition of data into k clusters.

00:29:35.549 --> 00:29:40.640
Now, as we discussed, k-means clustering does
not give us a hierarchy among clusters.

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:44.340
If we change k, the memberships will change.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:50.090
k-means also works best when we have compact
spherical clusters with somewhat comparable

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:51.529
number of members.

00:29:51.529 --> 00:29:56.340
So in that regard, k-means behavior, a lot
of times, is somewhat similar to complete

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.620
linkage clustering.

00:29:59.620 --> 00:30:03.919
And one of the issues that might occur with
k-means clustering is that it may result in

00:30:03.919 --> 00:30:06.200
small empty clusters.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:09.600
And this could be potentially due to presence
of outliers.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:18.240
A remedy for this is to remove those empty
clusters and refit the partition with the

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:19.240
algorithm.

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:22.269
In a lot of cases, that improves the solution.

00:30:22.269 --> 00:30:23.269
But nonetheless, this could occur.

00:30:23.269 --> 00:30:29.300
Now, here are some examples of settings where
k-meas could work well.

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:35.259
In this case, k-means does a good job of partitioning
the observation to 4 sets.

00:30:35.259 --> 00:30:41.970
It's just a partition, whereas single linkage
clustering gives just one big cluster, and

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:47.440
then observations on the periphery that are
just being distinguished from those.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:53.110
Similarly, k-means clustering would be able
to separate these two clusters from each other,

00:30:53.110 --> 00:30:59.000
whereas single linkage clustering, because
of this somewhat overlap between the two clusters,

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:00.700
won't be able to separate them from each other.

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:04.030
And it identifies these artificial clusters.

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:10.950
On the other hand, k-means, as we discussed,
prefers spherical distributions.

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:17.279
So it has difficulty identifying these embedded
clusters, whereas single linkage does a good

00:31:17.279 --> 00:31:18.740
job of that.

00:31:18.740 --> 00:31:23.650
And also, again in this example, k-means can't
distinguish between these two clusters, whereas

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:28.580
single linkage can do a good job in that example.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:35.720
Now, in k-means, we discussed that we need
to know the number of clusters, but that's

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.390
also somewhat true about hierarchical clustering.

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:42.610
We need to decide where to cut the dendrogram
and how many clusters to include.

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:44.950
In general, this is a difficult question.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:53.000
Like many other unsupervised learning tasks,
we need a validation set approach.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:57.990
And these approaches don't work directly in
unsupervised learning techniques, unlike supervised

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:02.309
learning where we could do cross-validation,
for example, in order to choose the tuning

00:32:02.309 --> 00:32:08.179
parameter in k-means or hierarchical clustering
if it's not trivial and it is somewhat difficult.

00:32:08.179 --> 00:32:09.909
There are some guidelines.

00:32:09.909 --> 00:32:14.180
For example, as we discussed in hierarchical
clustering, we could examine the dendrogram,

00:32:14.180 --> 00:32:18.590
see where the gaps are in the dendrogram,
and use those in order to identify the right

00:32:18.590 --> 00:32:19.590
number of clusters.

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:22.649
There are also some guidelines for k-means
clustering.

00:32:22.649 --> 00:32:27.139
We could vary the number of clusters.

00:32:27.139 --> 00:32:34.830
We could vary k and then look at the value
of the objective function over different k's.

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:39.929
And then the guideline is that we should be
looking at the, quote unquote, "elbow" in

00:32:39.929 --> 00:32:45.470
the plot, meaning that, usually the value
of the objective function drops down quickly

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:46.960
and then it flattens out.

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:53.370
And then the spot where we see that the flattening
of the curve of the objective function usually

00:32:53.370 --> 00:32:56.940
corresponds to a right number of clusters
for the data.

00:32:56.940 --> 00:33:04.400
But again, these are somewhat arbitrary, and
they're not very rigorous guidelines.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:10.140
Now, probably the best possible way to find
the number of clusters is again to go back

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:16.370
to the fact that hierarchical clustering,
k-means clustering, general clustering, and

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:19.690
unsupervised learning are parts of the data
analysis pipeline.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:28.809
So we could, in fact, consider varying the
value of k, or the number of clusters, and

00:33:28.809 --> 00:33:32.049
see the effect of it on the downstream analyses.

00:33:32.049 --> 00:33:38.720
Oftentimes, as we discussed, we use unsupervised
learning to further to try to identify some

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:43.649
patterns and then use supervised learning
techniques later on in order to validate these

00:33:43.649 --> 00:33:47.389
or in order to do some downstream analysis.

00:33:47.389 --> 00:33:51.240
And the best possible way to identify what
is the right number of clusters for that dataset

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:56.909
that you're analyzing is to use a validation
set approach or a training test approach in

00:33:56.909 --> 00:34:01.850
conjunction with that supervised technique
to, at the end, determine what is the appropriate

00:34:01.850 --> 00:34:05.960
number of clusters that you should consider.

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:09.919
There are some challenges and caveats with
clustering, as we discussed.

00:34:09.919 --> 00:34:18.120
There is, of course, no one size fits all
solution for clustering, or even a good definition

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:22.270
of what clusterings should look like.

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:28.450
There's not a single best criterion for obtaining
a partition because, as we discussed, the

00:34:28.450 --> 00:34:32.660
definition is, by design, somewhat vague.

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:38.271
In biomedical big data, clusters could have
arbitrary shapes and sizes, and we don't have

00:34:38.271 --> 00:34:44.240
a way of identifying what are the shapes and
sizes of clusters or understanding what the

00:34:44.240 --> 00:34:46.890
shapes and sizes of clusters.

00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:51.680
So we won't be able to distinguish these from
each other.

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:55.630
And it is very hard to evaluate how well a
clustering algorithm performs, especially

00:34:55.630 --> 00:35:00.000
when we are working with biomedical big data.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:06.580
So these are the issues that come up when
we try to validate and invalidate clustering

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:07.580
results.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.910
And we should keep in mind that, again, the
best way to do this would be just to view

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:17.990
clustering as part of the data analysis pipeline
and validate it at that perspective.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:23.960
The final point I want to emphasize on is
that the preprocessing steps, the data wrangling

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:30.270
steps, including centering and scaling for
example, could affect the result of clustering.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:37.320
So again, we should see cautioning as a step
in that whole process and think about the

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:45.670
consequences for example whether we center
or scale the observations or variables and

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:51.860
the effect that that would have on the clustering
algorithms.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:57.050
So now moving on from clustering to dimension
reduction.

00:35:57.050 --> 00:36:01.770
As we discussed, the goal of clustering is
to identify homogeneous subgroups in the data.

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:05.810
On the other hand, dimension reduction methods
try to find low dimensional representation

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:10.050
of the data that maintain important features
of the day.

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:16.770
Today, we will only talk about probably the
most widely used dimension reduction technique,

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.930
which is the Principal Components Analysis,
or PCA.

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:21.890
But there are many other choices out there.

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:28.400
We won't talk about mult-dimensional scaling,
independent components analysis, self-organizing

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:33.152
maps, and non-negative matrix factorization,
and many variants of these methods.

00:36:33.152 --> 00:36:39.020
This is a growing and active area of research,
but we will just talk about the basic concepts

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:41.530
using PCA as an example.

00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:45.020
Now, why do we use dimension reduction?

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:48.360
There are many reasons to do dimension reduction.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:51.940
For example, in high dimensions, data visualization
becomes very difficult.

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:58.950
We can't really plot many two dimensional
scatter plots if we have large number of features,

00:36:58.950 --> 00:36:59.950
p.

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:05.340
We would want to reduce the dimensionality
in order to aid visualization for example.

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:08.330
Often, there's high degree of redundancy in
the data.

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:12.760
there's correlational among features, so we
don't need to have all of the features in

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:19.790
order to understand the properties of the
data or characteristics of the data.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:25.720
There are many features in any study that
may not be informative for that particular

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:26.720
study.

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:30.750
You could think of these as somewhat noise
features.

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:39.250
And it would be helpful to somewhat reduce
the effect of these features and come up with

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:48.790
dimension reductions that would correspond
to easier analyses.

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:54.200
And dimension reduction techniques can retain
important features of the data while reducing

00:37:54.200 --> 00:38:00.190
these noise features and also redundancy in
our analysis, both to aid visualization and

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:07.290
also to help with supervised learning techniques,
as we will discuss later.

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:14.420
So the most well known dimension reduction
method, dimension in principal components

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:20.340
analysis, or PCA-- the idea is that we start
with data matrix, x, the same data matrix

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:21.900
we had at the beginning.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:29.990
This is an n by p array of value for n subjects
and p features.

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:32.470
And the idea is that not all these features
are needed.

00:38:32.470 --> 00:38:37.970
You might be able to reduce the dimension.

00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:43.630
And the goal of PCA is to find a lower dimensional
presentation of the data that captures most

00:38:43.630 --> 00:38:45.230
of the information in the data.

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:47.930
And PCA is widely used in biomedical big data.

00:38:47.930 --> 00:38:53.540
For example, in population genetics in genome-wide
association studies, PCA is used to correct

00:38:53.540 --> 00:38:55.210
for stratification.

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:58.860
It's used to study patterns of human migration.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:05.380
And also, it's used to identify and correct
batch effects.

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:11.640
In last week's webinar, it was discussed that
batch effects would have severe consequences

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:14.290
on the performance of supervised learning
techniques.

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:19.670
And usually as a pre-processing step, we need
to identify these artificial effects, or batch

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:27.530
effects, or artificial nonbiological effects
that are results of data acquisition or processing

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:28.700
steps.

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:33.560
And it would be important to identify these
and try to correct for these so that we have

00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:39.260
a data set that truly represents important
biological information.

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:42.920
And one of the main tools and techniques for
identifying and

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:48.290
correcting batch effects is based on PCA.

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:54.200
So to explain the main idea of PCA, again,
let's look at a simple example, with two features,

00:39:54.200 --> 00:40:01.810
x1 and x2, and some observations in this scatterplot
of two features.

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:07.770
Now, the goal of PCA, as we discussed, is
whether we can find a lower dimensional representation.

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:12.150
So for example, whether we could find a good
one dimensional representation of the data

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.210
in this example.

00:40:14.210 --> 00:40:17.040
Well, we have some choices.

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:19.190
We could use one of the two variables.

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:25.100
So we could use either x1 or x2 as our dimension
reduction.

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:28.840
Probably a better choice to use a linear combination
of the two.

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:36.940
So we could form a new variable, z1, that
is a linear combination of x1 and x2-- basically,

00:40:36.940 --> 00:40:39.550
a way to average the two variables.

00:40:39.550 --> 00:40:44.040
So this would correspond to, basically a new
direction, depending on the choices of w1

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:46.250
and w2, we would get different lines.

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:50.690
And then the question is, how, do we choose
these weights, w1 and w2.

00:40:50.690 --> 00:40:57.130
And PCA's answer to this question is that
the direction that is most informative is

00:40:57.130 --> 00:41:00.870
a direction that captures the majority of
the variance in the data.

00:41:00.870 --> 00:41:10.170
In PCA, we look to find the direction that
captures most of the variance in the data

00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:14.200
or most of the variation in the data.

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:21.760
So in this toy example, that corresponds to
the direction of maximum correlation between

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:24.060
x1 and x2.

00:41:24.060 --> 00:41:26.430
Now what if we needed another direction?

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:30.620
Suppose that we wanted another direction,
z2, that was another linear combination of

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:32.540
x1 and x2.

00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:37.490
Well, to find additional principal components--
and these directions are referred to as principal

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:42.980
components-- we choose the subsequent linear
combinations that are orthogonal or perpendicular

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:44.340
to the previous ones.

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:51.020
So once we found this direction-- again, it's
called a principal component, or PC-- once

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:57.370
we found this direction, we try to find future
directions that are orthogonal to the previous

00:41:57.370 --> 00:41:58.370
ones.

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:03.470
So what that means is that we will choose
the vector v of weights that is orthogonal

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:07.990
to the previous vector w, but still explains
the majority of variables in the data.

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:11.970
Of course, in the two dimensional setting,
once we find the vector, v, that is orthogonal

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:15.220
to w, then we don't have any choice.

00:42:15.220 --> 00:42:17.060
That defines the vector.

00:42:17.060 --> 00:42:18.610
This is always the case for the last PC.

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:24.660
The last PC is defined directly by the fact
that it's perpendicular to all the previous

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:25.660
ones.

00:42:25.660 --> 00:42:31.520
However, if p is larger than 2, then we have
many choices for the second PC.

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:35.650
And then we would choose among those the one
that explains the maximum variation in the

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:39.650
data and is orthogonal to the previous ones.

00:42:39.650 --> 00:42:47.170
So in this example, again, this choice of
second PC is somewhat clear.

00:42:47.170 --> 00:42:49.360
It's the one that is orthogonal to the first
one.

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:55.930
And then basically, when you look at this
plot, based on the two principal components,

00:42:55.930 --> 00:43:02.070
PCA just defines a new coordinate system for
the observations that now you could think

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:04.371
of plotting the observation along this new
co

00:43:04.371 --> 00:43:09.620
ordinate system instead of the original coordinates
system.

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:13.050
And that's, a lot of times, how we use PCA
in order to do data visualizations in high

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:15.450
dimensions.

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:28.090
If we had higher dimensions when we have p
larger than 2, PCA again defines a lower dimensional

00:43:28.090 --> 00:43:29.090
representation.

00:43:29.090 --> 00:43:33.780
And in fact, it defines a lower dimensional
representation that is the best approximation

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:34.780
to the data.

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:41.230
So we can define this two dimensional approximation
of the data where we project each one of the

00:43:41.230 --> 00:43:43.920
observations into this plane.

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:46.480
And that would give us the PCA solution.

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:50.520
So the PCA solution with the first principle
component and the second principle component

00:43:50.520 --> 00:43:58.910
captures the majority of variability that
could be captured by a two dimensional representation.

00:43:58.910 --> 00:44:04.700
This is the best approximation to he data
that we can get.

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:11.950
So here is a classical example of a application
of PCA on a data set that was collected back

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:13.730
in 1973.

00:44:13.730 --> 00:44:21.550
And these are crime statistics in arrests
per 100,000 residents for solved murder rate

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:26.310
along with the percentage of urban population
in the 50 United States.

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:31.570
As we can see from the two dimensional scatter
plots, there is some correlation between some

00:44:31.570 --> 00:44:32.570
of these crime statistics.

00:44:32.570 --> 00:44:37.330
Murder and assault, for example, are correlated
with each other.

00:44:37.330 --> 00:44:41.690
There's some correlation between assault and
rape and assault and murder.

00:44:41.690 --> 00:44:45.790
And urban population is less correlated with
these two.

00:44:45.790 --> 00:44:48.190
But nonetheless, there is some correlation
in the data.

00:44:48.190 --> 00:44:52.330
Now, if we apply PCA-- I won't go into detail
of how we get these.

00:44:52.330 --> 00:44:57.500
If we apply PCA and then plot the observation
of the space of the first two PCs, we see

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:03.230
some interesting patterns just using two principal
components.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:09.970
So instead of now having four variables, we've
reduced the dimensionality into two.

00:45:09.970 --> 00:45:14.710
And that allows us to do visualization, for
example, and identification of interesting

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:15.980
patterns.

00:45:15.980 --> 00:45:22.290
So in the space of the first two PCs for example,
we have the observations plotted.

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:28.770
And in this particular plot, which is referred
to as a biplot, we also have the variables

00:45:28.770 --> 00:45:32.520
or features overlaid in this spot using these
arrows.

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:38.120
So this shows that basically the first principal
component is somewhat of an average of all

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:39.120
four variables.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:41.400
As we saw, these variables were correlated
with each other.

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:45.790
So the first principle components in these
settings usually captures an overall average

00:45:45.790 --> 00:45:46.830
of the variables.

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:52.360
So long as the first principle components
has been moved to the right, we have higher

00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:58.160
levels of some combination of urban population,
rape, assault and murder in the states that

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:05.840
are on the right and a lower values of those
in the states that are in the far left.

00:46:05.840 --> 00:46:14.280
Whereas the second principle component direction
tells us something interesting about the contrast

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:19.590
between assault and murder that have negative
values on this second principle component

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:27.440
versus urban population and rape that have
positive values in this second principle component.

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:42.620
So basically, states that are at the bottom
half of this second principle component are

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:47.230
those that have higher rates of arrest due
to assault and murder.

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:56.090
And states that are in the top half of the
plots were based on the second principal component

00:46:56.090 --> 00:47:03.010
are those that have higher rates of arrest
based on rape and also have higher urban population

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:04.170
in those states.

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:09.170
So already, this plot gives us some really
interesting information about the data that

00:47:09.170 --> 00:47:15.740
we could not just see based on the 2D scatterplots.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:20.911
Just plotting everything in the space of the
first two PCs in addition to dimension reduction

00:47:20.911 --> 00:47:29.940
gives us a way of identifying these interesting
features.

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:34.930
Now again, the question that comes up is that
how many principal components should be used.

00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:39.940
As before, this is somewhat tricky question
to answer.

00:47:39.940 --> 00:47:46.660
On the one hand, by increasing the number
of principal components, we would include

00:47:46.660 --> 00:47:49.060
more and more variation of the data.

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:50.820
We explain more of the variation in the data.

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:55.230
On the other hand, if we use too many PCs,
we don't get the advantage and benefits of

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:57.700
dimension reduction.

00:47:57.700 --> 00:48:06.720
Ideally, we want the smallest number of PCs
that capture the important information of

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:07.720
the data.

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:10.350
Unfortunately, this is not easy, as we discussed.

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:16.220
And again, the best way to achieve this, although
there are some guidelines and some ideas about

00:48:16.220 --> 00:48:23.420
how we could choose the number of PCs is to
think about PCA as part of unsupervised learning

00:48:23.420 --> 00:48:30.120
in the data analysis pipeline, and try to
identify the number of clusters that is best

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:33.650
based on that.

00:48:33.650 --> 00:48:40.770
As one example, in last week's webinar, one
of the methods that was discussed was principal

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:46.510
component regression, which is basically applying
PCA to first obtain a lower dimensional representation

00:48:46.510 --> 00:48:54.170
of the data, and then apply either regression
or classification on that space in order to

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:58.950
predict the outcome or find associations with
the features in that space.

00:48:58.950 --> 00:49:03.390
And this is a very powerful tool, especially
for prediction.

00:49:03.390 --> 00:49:10.200
It's not as great for finding association
because you are now in this lower dimensional

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:15.750
space that is the combination of the previous
variables.

00:49:15.750 --> 00:49:21.360
But nonetheless, a really good way of identifying
how many principal components we should include

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:27.070
is to look at the outcome of that regression
or classification at the end, and then try

00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:31.460
to use cross-validation, for example, in order
to identify how many principal components

00:49:31.460 --> 00:49:39.420
should be used in order to get the best prediction
in the test data.

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:44.690
To summarize, I want to emphasize-- again,
I know that I've mentioned this a couple times

00:49:44.690 --> 00:49:47.920
and I've repeated this over and over, but
this is really important.

00:49:47.920 --> 00:49:52.120
And unsupervised learning is just one step
in the data analysis pipeline.

00:49:52.120 --> 00:49:58.340
So we should see it as part of the data analysis
pipeline.

00:49:58.340 --> 00:50:04.430
And the results of unsupervised learning should
be ideally validated in that pipeline.

00:50:04.430 --> 00:50:10.830
We widely use unsupervised learning techniques
to preprocess data.

00:50:10.830 --> 00:50:15.930
So it's used in exploratory data analysis
to aid data visualization, data wrangling.

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:20.080
For example, as we discussed, its used to
correct for batch effects.

00:50:20.080 --> 00:50:24.180
And then, its also used for data visualization,
as we saw some examples of it.

00:50:24.180 --> 00:50:27.140
It's used to extract features and discover
patterns in the data.

00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:33.230
So all of this was part of exploratory data
analysis, and we use it to generate new hypotheses.

00:50:33.230 --> 00:50:39.460
But it's also used in confirmatory data analysis
as we just discussed in the example of a principal

00:50:39.460 --> 00:50:40.460
component regression.

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:44.940
These unsupervised learning techniques are
used to improve the performance of supervised

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:49.470
techniques or in conjunction with supervised
learning techniques in confirmatory data analysis.

00:50:49.470 --> 00:50:56.240
The selection of tuning parameters and validation
of the results of unsupervised learning techniques

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:59.020
is often difficult.

00:50:59.020 --> 00:51:03.550
This is partly because we don't have a response--
we don't have an outcome to use, for example,

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:05.080
in order to do cross-validation.

00:51:05.080 --> 00:51:11.860
However, if we see it again as part of the
whole pipeline, we would be able to use the

00:51:11.860 --> 00:51:13.730
outcome of the analysis.

00:51:13.730 --> 00:51:21.200
If this is not feasible, if there is truly
no outcome, then what you would want to do

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:25.750
is to try to validate what we find in an independent
dataset.

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:30.360
So let's say we get some clusterings, and
we should repeat the clustering on an independent

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:35.600
data to see if clusterings that we get are
somewhat similar, or if we use that clustering

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:42.930
from the previous study, whether those correspond
to interesting patterns in the new dataset

00:51:42.930 --> 00:51:44.000
as well.

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:50.070
And especially, when we are using unsupervised
learning techniques purely as exploratory

00:51:50.070 --> 00:51:55.440
data analysis tools, we should be cautious
about interpreting the results of those analyses.

00:51:55.440 --> 00:52:01.150
We should keep in mind that without external
validation, there is danger that we might

00:52:01.150 --> 00:52:09.040
not be able to produce the findings of our
exploratory data analysis and what we are

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:19.190
getting could be potentially just features
that we have captured by looking at the data.

00:52:19.190 --> 00:52:26.280
And they might not be generalizable to other
datasets and other studies.

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:28.950
And with that, I want to thank you for listening.

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:32.070
And I'm happy to answer any questions that
they might have.

00:52:32.070 --> 00:52:33.070
Thank you, so much Ali.

00:52:33.070 --> 00:52:37.980
That was a really great overview on unsupervised
machine learning.

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:43.240
I do want to encourage folks who are in our
audience, if you have any questions to use

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:46.790
the little question submission system to send
in a few questions.

00:52:46.790 --> 00:52:50.780
And I'll try and get to as many as we can
before the top of the hour.

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:57.130
One question, for you, Ali, is you had made--
in your discussion on the creation of the

00:52:57.130 --> 00:53:02.820
dendrograms in your clustering algorithm discussion,
you mentioned measures of distance.

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:07.740
Are there any caveats about the choice of
the measure of distance, whether it's a Euclidean

00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:13.180
metric, whether it's like a city block metric,
or perhaps even an information criteria that

00:53:13.180 --> 00:53:16.460
one needs to worry about at all?

00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:17.540
Absolutely.

00:53:17.540 --> 00:53:19.320
So there are many choices as you mentioned.

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:24.250
Some of them-- there are many choices of distance
or similarity.

00:53:24.250 --> 00:53:26.690
And the main caveat is that there is no best
measure.

00:53:26.690 --> 00:53:34.110
That different datasets and different depending
on what are the clusterings within the data,

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:37.010
different measures might work better in different
situations.

00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:41.960
And unfortunately, that's another thing that
we won't know.

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:49.830
And the best way to do clustering is to often
try different metrics and try different similarity

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:54.910
or distance measures
and see which one of them validates better.

00:53:54.910 --> 00:53:59.140
So again, I want to go back to this idea that
unsupervised learning is part of the pipeline.

00:53:59.140 --> 00:54:04.220
Which one of them gives us results that are
more generalizable if we look at the test

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:06.950
set.

00:54:06.950 --> 00:54:15.800
So ideally we should try a couple of different
choices of distance or similarity to see which

00:54:15.800 --> 00:54:20.700
one of them gives us more generalizable results.

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:22.150
OK.

00:54:22.150 --> 00:54:23.600
Great.

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:28.550
Well, one other question was in principal
components analysis.

00:54:28.550 --> 00:54:34.040
Are there any recommended axis rotation techniques
which might be useful in helping to make your

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:36.700
results more interpretable?

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:45.810
Well, so principal component is, by design,
a way of rotating the axes.

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:53.050
So basically, going back to the slide I had
on principal components, basically what principal

00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:56.650
components does is that it gives you a new
coordinate system.

00:54:56.650 --> 00:54:59.030
Basically, it's a rotation of the original
data.

00:54:59.030 --> 00:55:05.070
Now, in addition to rotation, in a higher
dimension, you're getting this rotation in

00:55:05.070 --> 00:55:08.960
a reduced dimension.

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:13.680
So instead of having a three dimensional space,
you have a two dimensional space.

00:55:13.680 --> 00:55:23.190
But principal components already tries to
find the optimal rotation 

00:55:23.190 --> 00:55:25.190
that captures the information in the data.

00:55:25.190 --> 00:55:28.570
The feature of the principal component-- the
property of principal component is that the

00:55:28.570 --> 00:55:31.250
two axes are orthogonal to each other.

00:55:31.250 --> 00:55:36.950
So that helps open up the correlation basically
between observations.

00:55:36.950 --> 00:55:43.940
So instead of having observations plotted
along the correlation, as we had x1 and x2,

00:55:43.940 --> 00:55:49.230
now, if we look at this new coordinate system--
in this new coordinate system, the observations

00:55:49.230 --> 00:55:53.380
can be opened up from each other more easily.

00:55:53.380 --> 00:56:00.720
So I would say just looking at principal components
usually gives us the right rotation of the

00:56:00.720 --> 00:56:01.720
data.

00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:05.720
A lot of times the question is how many of
these PCs should be used or which one of the

00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:06.940
PCs should we look at.

00:56:06.940 --> 00:56:13.650
Should we look at PC1 1 and PC 2 or should
we look at PC 1 and PC 3 if you are doing

00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:14.650
2D scatterplots.

00:56:14.650 --> 00:56:19.420
So that's usually the more difficult question
to answer.

00:56:19.420 --> 00:56:22.960
So you should inspect your data, I guess,
is inspects the results-

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:24.260
Yes.
--and that will tell you-

00:56:24.260 --> 00:56:28.210
Yes.
--which kind of brings me to one of the questions

00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:33.640
from our audience is there seems to be a lot
of supervision in unsupervised machine learning.

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:41.570
How do you kind of strike a balance between
how much inputs or trial and error that a

00:56:41.570 --> 00:56:46.080
user might input with letting the machine
do what it does?

00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:48.650
So that's actually a really good question.

00:56:48.650 --> 00:56:54.110
In general, I would say that unsupervised
learning is one of the areas

00:56:54.110 --> 00:57:04.500
that supervision, if you will, or involvement
of the data scientists is somewhat critical.

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:12.770
Many of the unsupervised learning techniques
are harder to put into an automatic framework.

00:57:12.770 --> 00:57:17.740
A lot of times, there is need for data scientists
to be engaged.

00:57:17.740 --> 00:57:25.670
And this is a really good opportunity, for
example, for interaction from data scientists

00:57:25.670 --> 00:57:28.700
with the data at see what are the interesting
features.

00:57:28.700 --> 00:57:34.930
We don't want to do this to the extent that
we are basically finding artificial patterns

00:57:34.930 --> 00:57:36.400
in the data.

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:41.880
And again, that goes back to finding patterns
that are generalizable at a test dataset or

00:57:41.880 --> 00:57:43.930
in cross-validation.

00:57:43.930 --> 00:57:51.090
But a lot of times, unsupervised learning
techniques do require some involvement by

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:53.340
the data scientist.

00:57:53.340 --> 00:58:00.810
So how does that-- your interest is in building
a model of the data, are there different approaches

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:06.560
that you need to be considering between the
use of a supervised approach versus an unsupervised

00:58:06.560 --> 00:58:09.010
learning approach?

00:58:09.010 --> 00:58:14.540
Well, of course.

00:58:14.540 --> 00:58:21.430
We use unsupervised techniques, a lot of times,
to gain insight into what are the characteristics

00:58:21.430 --> 00:58:23.880
of the data, what are the features of the
data.

00:58:23.880 --> 00:58:29.480
And those will help us determine what are
the right models-- what are the proper unsupervised

00:58:29.480 --> 00:58:31.470
learning techniques.

00:58:31.470 --> 00:58:34.060
So that's one way that these tools are used
together.

00:58:34.060 --> 00:58:38.430
The other way, as I briefly discussed in the
case of principal component regression is

00:58:38.430 --> 00:58:44.280
that sometimes unsupervised learning techniques
are used in order to aid supervised learning

00:58:44.280 --> 00:58:48.850
techniques-- in order to improve the performance
of supervised learning techniques.

00:58:48.850 --> 00:58:54.430
So the final model is basically using unsupervised
learning to first reduce the dimension.

00:58:54.430 --> 00:59:01.860
And then we build a prediction model or, let's
say, a regression model.

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:07.660
So we could use these in conjunction, and
use them together in order to get a model

00:59:07.660 --> 00:59:10.460
that is most appropriate.

00:59:10.460 --> 00:59:16.140
But the answer, to some extent, depends on
what is the goal of our analysis.

00:59:16.140 --> 00:59:23.220
So if the goal is purely prediction, we could
use principal component regression.

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:27.510
And PCA together with regression or prediction.

00:59:27.510 --> 00:59:32.940
However, if the goal is to find association
between the original features and the outcome,

00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:37.190
that technically doesn't work very well because
each one of the PCs is a linear combination

00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:39.370
of all the original features.

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:46.280
So we can't use principal component regression
in order to identify which one of the features

00:59:46.280 --> 00:59:48.070
is associated with the outcome.

00:59:48.070 --> 00:59:50.930
So the answer to this question somewhat depends
on what is the goal of our analysis.

00:59:50.930 --> 00:59:57.140
Do we want to do a good job of prediction
or do we want to find association between

00:59:57.140 --> 00:59:58.820
the features and the outcome?

00:59:58.820 --> 01:00:05.170
And once we answer that question, it will
determine what are the right choices of combinations

01:00:05.170 --> 01:00:09.600
of supervised and unsupervised learning techniques.

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:11.490
Well, fantastic.

01:00:11.490 --> 01:00:13.710
Well, we've run out of time.

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:16.190
And so we need to kind of sign off now.

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:20.150
But I want to thank Ali for his talk today.

01:00:20.150 --> 01:00:24.280
It was a really fantastic overview of unsupervised
machine learning.

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:26.600
I want to thank those of you who signed in.

01:00:26.600 --> 01:00:28.790
And thank you for your questions.

01:00:28.790 --> 01:00:32.510
And we'll look forward to talking to you again
next week.

01:00:32.510 --> 01:00:35.190
Once again, thank you,
Ali.

01:00:35.190 --> 01:00:39.620
Enjoy your weekend, everybody.

01:00:39.620 --> 01:00:41.830
Thank you.

